Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 13:52:36 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 4/5] amcp: CHANNEL ADD / CHANNEL REMOVE / VIDEO-MODE ADD at
 runtime (WO-1020)

New commands (global, general queue):
  CHANNEL ADD <video-mode> [8|16 | DEPTH n] [BT709|BT2020] [AT <n>]
      -> 201 CHANNEL ADD OK + the new channel number. Appends after the highest number
         ever used; AT n fills the empty slot n left by a REMOVE (or appends at n = next).
  CHANNEL REMOVE <n> [FORCE]
      -> 202. 403 while a route:// taken from n is live (after a 500 ms grace for routes
         whose producer is still on the destroyer thread), 503 if another client holds
         LOCK n, 401 for an unknown or already removed n. FORCE removes anyway and the
         routes go empty.
  VIDEO-MODE ADD <id> <w> <h> <time-scale> <duration> [1|2|P|I] [cadence ...]
      -> 202; same checks as <video-modes> (shared make_custom_video_format_desc, now also
         used at startup); same id + same parameters is a no-op, different ones 403.

Channel list:
- server/repository/queues share a copy-on-write channel_list. Readers take an immutable
  snapshot; ADD/REMOVE are serialised and publish a new vector. This replaces a
  std::vector that every AMCP thread read without a lock (repository :153/:199, batch
  iteration, ctx.channels->at()).
- A removed channel leaves an empty slot (default channel_context) so numbers never shift.
  Everything that iterates the list skips empty slots: INFO, CLEAR ALL, route:// lookup
  (get_channels), CHANNEL_GRID, batch stage_delayed setup; SWAP/LOCK naming an empty slot
  fail with 403.
- A channel command to an empty slot answers 401 at parse time; a non-channel command with
  a numeric first argument (CLS 3) still falls back to the global command.
- Each parsed command remembers the channel object it was parsed against. If the slot is
  empty or holds a different channel when it runs (REMOVE, or REMOVE + ADD AT), it fails
  with 403 instead of landing on the newcomer.
- AMCPProtocolStrategy (one per port + console, shared by all its connections) creates the
  per-channel queue on first use, so connections opened before a CHANNEL ADD can address
  the new channel without reconnecting.

Removal order (server.cpp remove_channel): empty the slot; close routes taken from it;
clear its LOCKs; drop all consumers (each on its own destroy thread, as AMCP REMOVE);
clear the stage (producers incl. HTML/CEF on the destroyer thread, as CLEAR; waited up to
5 s); release. The video_channel (thread join, mixer) is destroyed when the last snapshot
drops, normally this command's own, on the AMCP queue thread right after the reply.

INFO CONFIG: the boot tree with <channels> replaced by the live list: boot channels as
booted, runtime ones as <channel runtime="true"> with mode/depth/colour space, removed slots
as <channel removed="true"/> so positions still match channel numbers.

New channels are built by the same make_channel() as startup (image mixer at the current
maximum mode size, OSC tick under /channel/<n>, lock key lock<n>).

